source @arxiv/topology/hansen-ghrist-2018
source @arxiv/law/sel-v1.1
source @arxiv/practice/wolf-2026-07-14-ssh-signing-design-intent

prism @subject/visibility/sheaf {
  focus  sheaf_restriction
  project sheaf_restriction
  split  sheaf_restriction
  shift  sheaf_restriction
  settle sheaf_restriction
}

# === The sheaf_restriction carrier ===
#
# The sub-sheaf F_home|_{A_p} per peer-persistence §12.3 verbatim:
# the restriction of the home-repo sheaf to the vertices/edges the
# peer's ACL admits.
#
# Three fields:
#
#   peer_ref     — the subject_instance whose ACL defines the restriction.
#                  Ref into @subject; carries the two-witness discipline
#                  transitively (ssh_signature_fingerprint +
#                  spectral_signature_ref both required for the peer to
#                  be a licensable-party against whom a restriction is
#                  named). Per Alex 2026-07-14: the peer-key stays
#                  .git/mirror-side; this ref is the Fractal.Lens
#                  POINTER, not the key material.
#
#   acl          — the ACL admitting the sub-sheaf. Ref into @mirror/pack;
#                  acl is `type acl = ref` per pack.mirror line 123.
#                  The ACL structure (ops + targets + predicates per
#                  peer-ACL §5) determines which vertices/edges of the
#                  base graph the restriction admits.
#
#   admitted_stalks — the substrate-observable list of stalks the
#                  restriction admits. Ref into the base graph's
#                  stalk set filtered by the ACL; the byte-visible
#                  record of "what the peer can compute a section
#                  over." @epistemologic/math/sheaf_laplacian.
#                  restriction carriers compose OVER this at the
#                  realisation layer (each admitted stalk contributes
#                  one row/column block to the assembled Δ_F).
#
# Identity contract: byte-equality on the (peer_ref, acl,
# admitted_stalks) triple. Two sheaf_restriction values with the
# same peer_ref + acl but different admitted_stalks are DISTINCT —
# the admitted-stalk set is part of the substrate identity because
# it makes the sub-sheaf's admissible region byte-visible.
type sheaf_restriction = {
  peer_ref:        ref,
  acl:             ref,
  admitted_stalks: ref,
}

# === The section_at_stalk carrier ===
#
# The peer's computable visibility at one crystal's stalk under the
# restriction. Per peer-persistence §12.3 verbatim: "the peer's
# visibility scope for a given crystal is the SECTION of
# F_home|_{A_p} at that crystal's stalk."
#
# Three fields:
#
#   crystal_ref  — the crystal (@bauchladen) whose stalk the section
#                  reads. Ref into the crystal store; identity is
#                  content-addressed (per @kintsugi/store/git).
#
#   sheaf_ref    — the sheaf_restriction the section was computed
#                  UNDER. Ref-equality on this field binds the
#                  section to its ACL context; a section computed
#                  under a different restriction is a DIFFERENT
#                  section_at_stalk value even if the crystal_ref is
#                  identical.
#
#   projected_value — the section's projected value at the stalk.
#                  Ref carrying the substrate-observable content the
#                  peer's ACL admits; the realisation layer
#                  materialises the concrete bytes when @secrets /
#                  @secrets/sops discharge the key-gated projection
#                  through @io at Arc-2.3.
#
# Identity contract: byte-equality on the (crystal_ref, sheaf_ref,
# projected_value) triple.
type section_at_stalk = {
  crystal_ref:     ref,
  sheaf_ref:       ref,
  projected_value: ref,
}

# === restrict — the restriction operation ===
#
# Given a sheaf (ref into the substrate's cellular-sheaf carrier at
# @epistemologic/math/sheaf_laplacian) and an ACL, construct the
# sheaf_restriction admitting the ACL's substructure.
#
# Substrate-decl form of peer-persistence §12.3's F|_A construction.
# Composes over @epistemologic/math/sheaf_laplacian.restriction at
# the realisation layer: the returned sheaf_restriction.admitted_
# stalks references the sub-list of Δ_F row/column blocks the ACL's
# `targets` slot admits.
#
# THE peer-ACL projection primitive. Per shape proposal §2.2 example
# rewritten under the 5dcad39 collapse [Note: acl_project collapsed
# into restrict per Seam extended-scope etymology audit at 5dcad39
# §2.5.a Q_C — the ACL-projection variant was CS-brain redundancy;
# `restrict(F_home, peer_acl)` IS the geometric operation]:
#
#   sr := @subject/visibility/sheaf.restrict(F_home, peer.acl)
#
# Given F_home (the home-repo sheaf; ref into the substrate's
# cellular-sheaf carrier) and an ACL (typically the peer's ACL for
# Arc-2.3 peer_persistence composition), project the sub-sheaf the
# ACL admits. When the ACL is a peer's ACL, the peer_ref field of
# the returned sheaf_restriction resolves through @subject.pack.
# members to the subject_instance the ACL binds; the two-witness
# discipline is discharged transitively.
#
# Per Alex 2026-07-14 SSH-signing design intent: the peer's key
# material stays .git/mirror-side; @secrets / @secrets/sops discharge
# the key-gated projection through @io at the realisation layer;
# Fractal.Lens (the peer_ref field) is the POINTER, not the thing.
#
# Composes with @subject/visibility.filter at consumer altitude:
# once restrict returns the sheaf_restriction, section_at reads
# the per-crystal projected value; filter narrows the bauchladen tray
# to crystals whose section admits the viewer.
#
# Body discharges per-realisation. `\`-obligation-blocked per
# [[feedback-craft-not-deliver]]; Arc-1 evaluator FLOOR pending;
# Arc-2.3 landing composes this over @io.
restrict(sheaf: ref, acl: ref) -> sheaf_restriction { \ }

# === section_at — the section computation ===
#
# Given a sheaf_restriction and a crystal_ref, compute the section
# at the crystal's stalk under the restriction. Per §12.3 verbatim:
#
#   visibility_scope_p(crystal) := section_of(F_home|_{ACL_p},
#                                              crystal.stalk)
#
# Returns a section_at_stalk carrying the crystal_ref (identity),
# the sheaf_ref (which restriction the section was computed under),
# and the projected_value (the substrate-observable content the ACL
# admits at that stalk).
#
# Rice-safe bound: reads the admitted_stalks list (byte-visible list
# lookup); reads the base sheaf's per-stalk value (byte-visible ref
# resolution); no program semantics inspection required. Bounded in
# O(|admitted_stalks|) worst case; O(log |admitted_stalks|) with
# indexed lookup at the realisation layer.
#
# Body discharges per-realisation. `\`-obligation-blocked. Arc-2.3
# landing composes this over @secrets / @secrets/sops for key-gated
# projected_value materialisation.
section_at(F_A_p: sheaf_restriction, crystal_ref: ref) -> section_at_stalk { \ }

# === restriction_admissible — bilateral predicate ===
#
# Discharges Pass iff the sheaf_restriction is substrate-honest per
# peer-persistence §12.3 discipline. Three sub-checks (whole-tick
# altitude):
#
#   1. peer_ref resolves to a subject_instance whose
#      two_witness_verification passes (@subject discipline). A
#      restriction against a non-witnessed peer is refused at
#      substrate altitude.
#
#   2. acl resolves to a well-formed acl value per @mirror/pack
#      grammar (ops + targets + predicates slots parseable; per
#      peer-ACL §10 composition rules satisfied).
#
#   3. admitted_stalks is a SUB-set of the base sheaf's stalk set
#      (the ACL cannot admit stalks the base sheaf does not carry;
#      substrate-decl refuses hallucinated stalks).
#
# Fail iff any sub-check fails. Rice-safe at whole-tick altitude per
# Mara-B §4.5.5: reads only byte-visible empirical state (subject
# witness records, ACL byte structure, stalk set membership); no
# program semantics.
#
# Body discharges per-realisation. `\`-obligation-blocked.
restriction_admissible(sr: sheaf_restriction) -> verdict { \ }

# === section_admissible — bilateral predicate ===
#
# [Note: renamed from section_computable per Seam extended-scope
# etymology audit at 5dcad39 §2.5.b — `_computable` imported CS/
# computability-theory baggage; `_admissible` preserves the
# substrate geometric reading ("the ACL admits the stalk") AND
# matches the landed 15+ bilateral suffix pattern per AGENTS.md
# §7.5.]
#
# Discharges Pass iff the section_at_stalk is admissible under the
# section_at Rice-safe bound above — i.e., the ACL admits the
# crystal's stalk, so a section can be computed. Two sub-checks:
#
#   1. sheaf_ref resolves to a sheaf_restriction whose
#      restriction_admissible passes (transitively verifies the
#      restriction context).
#
#   2. crystal_ref's stalk is a member of sheaf_ref's
#      admitted_stalks (the ACL admits the crystal's stalk; a
#      section over a non-admitted stalk is NOT admissible at
#      substrate altitude and returns pending-boundary per
#      @kintsugi/consent Partial semantics).
#
# Fail iff either sub-check fails. Rice-safe at whole-tick altitude:
# reads only byte-visible state (transitive restriction_admissible
# state; stalk-set membership check).
#
# Load-bearing structural claim: section_admissible Pass is the
# substrate-decl form of "the peer's ACL admits this crystal." A
# section that is NOT admissible is a substrate-decl refusal of the
# read, not an error — the peer's visibility scope at this crystal is
# genuinely empty under the restriction.
#
# Body discharges per-realisation. `\`-obligation-blocked.
section_admissible(s: section_at_stalk) -> verdict { \ }

out @subject/visibility/sheaf
out sheaf_restriction
out section_at_stalk
out restrict
out section_at
out restriction_admissible
out section_admissible
